% =====================================================================
% refs.bib -- every entry verified to exist before it was written here.
%
% Amendment F4: "every citation must be checked to exist via web search or a
% known DOI; a fabricated reference is a D4-level halt."
%
% Verification method, per entry:
%   [DBLP]  matched against dblp.org's search API -- authors, venue, year,
%           pages and DOI copied from the returned record, not from memory.
%   [DOI]   the DOI was resolved through doi.org with
%           `Accept: application/x-bibtex` and returned HTTP 200 with a
%           record whose title and authors match.
%   [URL]   no DBLP/DOI record exists (vendor documentation); the live page
%           was fetched and the quoted text read off it on the stated date.
%
% Raw output of the sweep: reports/raw/f4-citation-verification.txt
% Re-run with: python scripts/verify_refs.py
%
% -- Two failures worth keeping, because both were silent --
%
% 1. A FABRICATED DOI. The ACM Queue version of Helland's idempotence article
%    was first written from memory as 10.1145/2181796.2229155. That DOI does
%    not resolve; doi.org returned HTTP 404 ("DOI Not Found") while the nine
%    real DOIs in this file all returned 200. The correct records are
%    10.1145/2160718.2160734 (CACM, used below) and 10.1145/2181796.2187821
%    (ACM Queue). A plausible-looking DOI is precisely what F4 is about, and
%    only the resolution check caught it.
%
% 2. COMMENTS INSIDE ENTRIES. The first version of this file put the
%    provenance note on the line after the entry's opening brace. BibTeX does
%    not accept "%" comments inside an entry: it reported "You're missing a
%    field name ... I'm skipping whatever remains of this entry" and emitted
%    24 EMPTY \bibitem blocks. The document still compiled and reported no
%    undefined citations, so nothing in the build said the bibliography was
%    blank. Provenance comments therefore live ABOVE each entry, never inside
%    one.
%
%    A second bite of the same apple: BibTeX's scanner treats an at-sign as
%    the start of an entry even on a line beginning with "%", so a comment
%    quoting an entry header silently swallowed the entry after it. No at-sign
%    appears in any comment in this file.
%
%    scripts/check_paper_numbers.py greps main.bbl for empty \bibitem blocks
%    and main.blg for BibTeX errors, so neither failure can recur silently.
% =====================================================================


